      "id": 1,
      "claim": "The dominant economic relationship between medieval English lords and peasants was feudal, based on obligatory labor services, rents, and dues rather than voluntary market exchange.",
      "evidence": "Manorial court rolls, Domesday Book entries, and scholarly works (e.g., Bloch 1961; Hilton 1975) describe peasants owing corvée labor, a portion of their harvest, and various fees to their lord.",
      "weight": 0.30,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Lords exercised legal control over local markets and monopolized essential facilities (mills, ovens, bakehouses), imposing fixed fees (banalities) on peasants.",
      "evidence": "Manorial records and royal charters granting market rights show lords could set prices and require peasants to use lord‑owned facilities at set rates (e.g., 13th‑century surveys of Kent and Sussex).",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Statutes such as the Statute of Labourers (1351) legally limited peasants’ wages and mobility, reinforcing lordly extraction.",
      "evidence": "The text of the Statute of Labourers and analyses by historians (e.g., Hatcher 1994) show it was enacted to curb rising wages after the Black Death, keeping labor cheap and bound to the manor.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Modern “fair trade” requires voluntary, informed, and equal bargaining power between autonomous parties, which feudal peasants lacked.",
      "evidence": "Fairtrade International defines fair trade as transactions where producers freely choose partners, receive equitable prices, and have legal protections; serfs were legally bound to the land, could not freely choose partners, and faced mandatory fees and wage caps.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [1, 2, 3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Because the necessary conditions for fair trade were absent, medieval English lords did not engage in fair trade with peasants.",
      "evidence": "Combining steps 1‑4 shows the relationship was coercive, monopolistic, and legally constrained, contradicting the core criteria of fair trade.",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": [1, 2, 3, 4]
    }
  ],
